.SH "NAME"
pdftex.cfg \- configuration settings for MiKTeX\-pdfTeX
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
MiKTeX\-pdfTeX
configurations settings are read from the file
pdftex\&.cfg
when a format file is being created by
MiKTeX\-pdfTeX\&.
.if n \{\
.sp
.\}
.RS 4
.it 1 an-trap
.nr an-no-space-flag 1
.nr an-break-flag 1
.br
.ps +1
\fBCaution\fR
.ps -1
.br
.PP
Do not edit this file directly\&. Run
\fBinitexmf \-\-edit\-config\-file pdftex\&.cfg\fR
to edit configuration settings for
MiKTeX\-pdfTeX\&.
.sp .5v
.RE
.SH "INSTRUCTIONS"
.PP
A typical
pdftex\&.cfg
file looks like this, setting up output for A4 paper size and the standard
TeX
offset of 1\ \&inch:
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
compress_level 9
decimal_digits 3
horigin 1 true in
vorigin 1 true in
image_resolution 300
move_chars 1
output_format 1
page_width 210 true mm
page_height 297 true mm
pdf_minorversion 4
pk_resolution 600
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.PP
The configuration file sets default values for these parameters, and they all can be overridden in the
TeX
source file\&. Dimensions can be specified as
\fBtrue\fR, which makes them immune for magnification (when set)\&.
.PP
compress_level
.RS 4
This integer parameter specifies the level of text and in||line graphics compression\&.
MiKTeX\-pdfTeX
uses Zip compression\&. A value of\ \&0 means no compression, 1\ \&means fastest, 9\ \&means best, 2\&.\&.8 means something in between\&. Just set this value to\ \&9, unless there is a good reason to do otherwise; 0 is great for testing macros that use
\fB\epdfliteral\fR\&.
.RE
.PP
decimal_digits
.RS 4
This integer specifies the preciseness of real numbers in
PDF
page descriptions\&. It gives the maximal number of decimal digits after the decimal point of real numbers\&. Valid values are in range 0\&.\&.5\&. A higher value means more precise output, but also results in a much larger file size and more time to display or print\&. In most cases the optimal value is\ \&2\&. This parameter does
\fInot\fR
influence the precision of numbers used in raw
PDF
code, like that used in
\fB\epdfliteral\fR
and annotation action specifications\&.
.RE
.PP
horigin & vorigin
.RS 4
These dimension parameters can be used to set the offset of the
TeX
output box from the top left corner of the
\(lqpaper\(rq\&.
.RE
.PP
image_resolution
.RS 4
When
MiKTeX\-pdfTeX
is not able to determine the natural dimensions of an image, it assumes a resolution of type 72\ \&dots per inch\&. Use this variable to change this default value\&.
.RE
.PP
move_chars
.RS 4
Although
PDF
output is claimed to be portable, especially when all font information is included in the file, problems with printing and viewing have a persistent nature\&. Moving the characters in range 0\(en31 sometimes helps a lot\&. When set to\ \&1, characters are only moved when a font has less than 128 glyphs, when set to\ \&2 higher slots are used too\&.
.RE
.PP
output_format
.RS 4
This integer parameter specifies whether the output format should be
DVI
or
PDF\&. A positive value means
PDF
output, otherwise we get
DVI
output\&.
.RE
.PP
page_width & page_height
.RS 4
These two dimension parameters
.\" paper size: changing
specify the output medium dimensions (the paper, screen or whatever the page is put on)\&. If they are not specified, these values are calculated\&.
.RE
.PP
pdf_minorversion
.RS 4
Sets the
PDF
version of the generated file and the latest allowed
PDF
version of included
PDFs\&. The value\ \&3 tells
MiKTeX\-pdfTeX
to set the
PDF
version to 1\&.3 and allows only included
PDFs with versions less than 1\&.3\&. A suitable default value is 4\&.
.RE
.PP
pk_resolution
.RS 4
One can use this entry to specify the resolution for bitmap fonts\&. Nowadays most printers are capable to print at least 600 dots per inch, so this is a reasonable default\&.
.RE
